/* Structured content from the user-supplied undergraduate lecture. Formula markup is generated locally; no remote mathematics runtime is required. */
window.FE_LECTURE={"title":"From the Received GNSS Signal to Digital Samples","sections":[{"number":1,"title":"Understanding the GNSS Signal","slides":[{"number":1,"title":"Introduction to the GNSS Front End","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A GNSS front end is the part of a satellite navigation receiver that receives, filters, amplifies, converts, and digitizes weak radio-frequency signals transmitted by navigation satellites."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The front end does not calculate the receiver's position. Its purpose is to prepare the received signals for subsequent digital signal processing."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning objectives"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Students should understand how a GNSS signal changes as it travels through the receiver and be able to identify the function of each front-end component."}]},{"number":2,"title":"What Is a GNSS Radio Signal?","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A GNSS radio signal is an electromagnetic wave transmitted by a navigation satellite. It carries information that allows a receiver to determine satellite-related timing and navigation parameters."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Three basic quantities describe the received electrical signal:"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Amplitude: The magnitude of the signal, represented by the height of its waveform."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Frequency: The number of complete oscillations occurring in one second."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Time: The quantity used to describe when the waveform changes or when measurements are taken."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show a simple sinusoidal waveform with time on the horizontal axis and amplitude on the vertical axis. Label one complete cycle."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This waveform is an introductory illustration of frequency, not a representation of the complete received GNSS signal."}]},{"number":3,"title":"Understanding the Time and Frequency Domains","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A time-domain graph shows how the amplitude of a signal changes with time."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A sinusoidal signal appears as a repeating waveform. A real received GNSS signal normally appears irregular because the satellite signal is extremely weak and is mixed with noise."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A frequency-domain graph shows how the signal's power is distributed over different frequencies."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For a GNSS signal, the frequency-domain representation identifies the carrier frequency, signal bandwidth, and unwanted frequency components."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"heading","text":"Time Domain"},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency Domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Conceptual representations of a single sinusoidal signal. Actual GNSS signals have a spread spectrum rather than a single narrow spectral line."}]},{"number":4,"title":"GPS L1 Carrier Frequency","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Carrier frequency is the frequency of the radio wave used to transmit the GNSS signal."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS L1 carrier frequency is:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{L1}=1575.42\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1575.42</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A frequency of 1575.42 MHz corresponds to approximately 1.575 billion cycles every second."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The corresponding wavelength is:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"\\lambda=\\frac{c}{f}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>\u03bb</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"\\lambda=\\frac{3\\times10^8}{1575.42\\times10^6}\n\\approx 0.1903\\text{ m}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>\u03bb</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>\u00d7</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1575.42</mn><mo>\u00d7</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>0.1903</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Therefore, the GPS L1 wavelength is approximately 19 cm."}]},{"number":5,"title":"How Information Is Carried by the GPS Signal","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS L1 C/A signal contains three important elements."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Carrier: The high-frequency wave transmitted at 1575.42 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"PRN ranging code: A known sequence of chips used to distinguish satellite signals and determine signal propagation delay. The GPS C/A code rate is 1.023 million chips per second."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Navigation data: Digital information describing satellite orbits, clock parameters, and related navigation information. The traditional GPS L1 C/A navigation data rate is 50 bits per second."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These elements are combined through modulation."}]},{"number":6,"title":"GPS Signal Modulation","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Modulation is the process of changing a carrier property to carry information."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS L1 C/A signal uses binary phase-shift keying (BPSK-like code modulation), in which the carrier phase changes by either 0\u00b0 or 180\u00b0."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider the sequence of code chips:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"+1,\\;-1,\\;+1","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The first chip preserves the reference carrier phase. The second reverses its sign, corresponding to a 180\u00b0 phase change. The third restores the original sign."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show three successive portions of a carrier waveform, with the middle portion inverted. Place the three chip values directly above the waveform."}]},{"number":7,"title":"Understanding GPS L1 Signal Bandwidth","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Bandwidth is the range of frequencies occupied by a signal or passed by a receiver component."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS L1 C/A signal has a chip rate of 1.023 million chips per second. Its approximate main-lobe null-to-null bandwidth is:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"BW=2(1.023)=2.046\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1.023</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2.046</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This main lobe extends approximately from:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"1574.397\\text{ MHz to }1576.443\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>1574.397</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz to </mtext><mn>1576.443</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"heading","text":"Important distinction"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The 2.046 MHz value describes the central main lobe, not the complete GPS L1 C/A spectrum. The complete spread-spectrum signal also contains weaker side lobes extending beyond the first nulls."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show the complete central main lobe and progressively smaller side lobes. Label the carrier frequency, the first two nulls, and the 2.046 MHz main-lobe width."}]},{"number":8,"title":"Why the GPS Signal Is Extremely Weak","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The satellite signal travels a very large distance before reaching the receiver. As a result, the received power is extremely small."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The source lecture uses a minimum GPS received signal power of approximately:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"P_r=-160\\text{ dBW}=-130\\text{ dBm}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>160</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dBW</mtext><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>130</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dBm</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For a receiver bandwidth of 2 MHz at approximately 290 K, the thermal noise power is:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"N=kTB","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"N\\approx-111\\text{ dBm}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>\u2248</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>111</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dBm</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Therefore, the example GPS signal is approximately 19 dB below the integrated thermal noise power."},{"type":"heading","text":"Interpretation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The received signal cannot normally be distinguished from thermal noise simply by observing the time-domain waveform."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A spectrum analyzer may also show noise dominating the weak GPS signal."}]},{"number":9,"title":"Visual Example: The Received Signal and Thermal Noise","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show two representations of the same incoming signal."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Time domain: An irregular waveform dominated by thermal noise, with no clearly identifiable satellite code."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Frequency domain: A broad noise floor with the desired GPS spectrum lying below or close to it. The central signal spectrum may not be directly visible in an ordinary short observation."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The explanation should emphasize that the satellite signal is still present, even when its waveform cannot be distinguished visually."}]}],"glossary":[["Radio Frequency (RF)","Frequency of an electromagnetic radio wave"],["Amplitude","Magnitude of a waveform"],["Frequency","Number of cycles per second"],["Carrier","Radio wave that carries modulated information"],["Modulation","Changing a carrier property to convey information"],["PRN Code","Known ranging-code sequence"],["Chip","One element of a ranging code"],["Bandwidth","Range of frequencies occupied or passed"],["Thermal Noise","Random electrical energy associated with temperature"],["Time Domain","Signal amplitude versus time"],["Frequency Domain","Signal power versus frequency"]]},{"number":2,"title":"Complete GNSS Front-End Architecture","slides":[{"number":10,"title":"The Complete GPS L1 Front-End Block Diagram","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use this same block diagram throughout the lecture, highlighting the relevant component when beginning each section."},{"type":"heading","text":"GPS L1 Reference Signal Chain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The diagram shows the main stages in order. The reference clock provides the frequency relationships needed for mixing and sampling."},{"type":"heading","text":"Reference design parameters"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Parameter\tValue"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"GPS L1 RF carrier\t1575.42 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Local Oscillator\t1527.68 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Analog Intermediate Frequency\t47.74 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sampling Frequency\t38.192 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Digital Intermediate Frequency\t9.548 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"ADC Resolution\t4 bits"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These values describe the reference implementation in the supplied lecture, not every possible GNSS receiver."}]},{"number":11,"title":"What Happens to the Signal Inside the Receiver?","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The front end performs the following operations in sequence."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Operation\tPurpose"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Reception\tConvert the arriving radio wave into an electrical signal"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"RF Filtering\tAttenuate unwanted frequencies"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Amplification\tIncrease signal magnitude while limiting added noise"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Frequency Translation\tShift the signal to a lower frequency"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"IF Filtering and Amplification\tControl bandwidth and prepare signal amplitude"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sampling\tMeasure the waveform at regular time intervals"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Quantization\tRepresent each measured amplitude using a finite number of levels"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The first five operations occur while the signal remains analog. Sampling and quantization take place in the ADC."}]}],"glossary":[]},{"number":3,"title":"GNSS Receiving Antenna","slides":[{"number":12,"title":"Function of the GNSS Antenna","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A GNSS receiving antenna converts incident electromagnetic waves into a weak electrical voltage that can enter the receiver's RF circuitry."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The antenna does not identify satellites or calculate coordinates."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"It receives the combined radio signals arriving from satellites within its supported frequency bands."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A receiver simultaneously receives signals from four GPS satellites."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"All four signals are collected by the same antenna. The receiver front end processes the combined signal, and the digital processing stages later distinguish the individual satellites using their PRN codes."}]},{"number":13,"title":"Antenna Polarization","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Polarization describes the orientation and movement of the electric field of a radio wave."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"GNSS satellites generally transmit right-hand circularly polarized (RHCP) signals. GNSS receiving antennas are designed for corresponding polarization."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A properly matched RHCP receiving antenna receives the direct GNSS signal more effectively than an ideal antenna designed only for the opposite circular polarization."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The matching of polarization reduces power loss."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Display an RHCP wave with the electric-field vector rotating along the direction of propagation."}]},{"number":14,"title":"Antenna Gain and Radiation Pattern","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Antenna gain describes how strongly the antenna receives signals from a particular direction."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Radiation pattern describes how this reception varies with direction."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A typical sky-facing GNSS patch antenna is designed to receive signals from satellites above the horizon while reducing reception from unwanted directions."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Compare a satellite directly overhead with one close to the horizon. Their signals may experience different antenna gains because the antenna response varies with elevation."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show a sky-facing antenna with a broad reception region above it and weaker reception toward the ground."}]},{"number":15,"title":"Multi-Constellation and Multi-Band Reception","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Multi-constellation reception means receiving and processing signals from more than one satellite navigation system."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"GPS L1, Galileo E1 and BeiDou B1C share the nominal center frequency of 1575.42 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Multi-band reception means supporting different frequency bands, such as GPS L1 at 1575.42 MHz and GPS L5 at 1176.45 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Multi-channel tracking means that the receiver's digital processor can track several satellite signals independently. It does not mean that each satellite requires its own antenna."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An L1-capable antenna receives signals from GPS and Galileo satellites together. After the front end digitizes the composite signal, different digital tracking channels identify and follow their respective satellite signals."}]},{"number":16,"title":"Active GNSS Antenna","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An active GNSS antenna includes a low-noise amplifier (LNA), which increases the weak received signal before substantial cable loss occurs."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reason for placing amplification near the antenna is to protect the received signal quality."},{"type":"heading","text":"Worked example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The lecture uses an antenna amplifier with 30 dB gain and a cable with 8 dB loss."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Assume the received signal power is \u2212130 dBm."},{"type":"formula","latex":"P_{\\mathrm{after\\ LNA}}=-130+30=-100\\text{ dBm}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>after LNA</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>130</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>100</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dBm</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"After the cable:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"P_{\\mathrm{after\\ cable}}=-100-8=-108\\text{ dBm}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>after cable</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>108</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dBm</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The cable reduces signal power, but the early amplifier gain makes the cable's noise contribution less significant than if the cable were placed ahead of the LNA."}]},{"number":17,"title":"Signal Condition After the GNSS Antenna","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The antenna produces an extremely weak analog electrical waveform. The waveform contains satellite signals, noise, and possible reflected signals."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"It does not resemble a clean sinusoidal signal in ordinary observation."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The received spectrum is centered around the supported GNSS frequencies. For the L1 example, the desired signal is centered at 1575.42 MHz, with its spread-spectrum bandwidth surrounding that frequency."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The signal may remain below the thermal noise floor."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Left: irregular low-amplitude analog waveform."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Right: noise spectrum with the weak L1 band marked at its correct frequency."}]}],"glossary":[["GNSS Antenna","Converts satellite radio waves into electrical signals"],["RHCP","Right-Hand Circular Polarization"],["Antenna Gain","Direction-dependent antenna reception strength"],["Radiation Pattern","Variation of antenna response with direction"],["Active Antenna","Antenna incorporating amplification"],["LNA","Low-Noise Amplifier"],["Multi-Constellation","Reception from different GNSS systems"],["Multi-Band","Reception across different frequency bands"],["Multi-Channel","Parallel digital tracking of satellite signals"]]},{"number":4,"title":"RF Bandpass Filter","slides":[{"number":18,"title":"Function of the RF Bandpass Filter","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An RF bandpass filter is a frequency-selective component that passes signals within a desired frequency range while reducing signals outside that range."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The received antenna signal may contain strong unwanted radio transmissions. These should be attenuated before they enter the high-gain receiver stages."},{"type":"heading","text":"Important point"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The RF filter does not separate individual GPS satellites. It selects a frequency band containing their combined signals."}]},{"number":19,"title":"Understanding Filter Bandwidth","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"A practical bandpass filter is described by its center frequency, bandwidth, insertion loss, and selectivity."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference RF filter uses:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_c=1575.42\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1575.42</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"BW_{3dB}\\approx50\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>50</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For a symmetric passband, the approximate lower and upper 3-dB frequencies are 1550.42 MHz and 1600.42 MHz."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS L1 signal falls within this passband, while radio signals sufficiently far outside it are strongly attenuated."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The transition is gradual: a real filter cannot perfectly pass every wanted frequency and completely block every unwanted frequency."}]},{"number":20,"title":"Insertion Loss of a Filter","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Insertion loss is the reduction of wanted signal power caused by the filter itself."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Even within the passband, a practical filter introduces some attenuation."},{"type":"heading","text":"Worked example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Suppose the signal entering a filter has power \u2212130 dBm and the filter has 1.5 dB insertion loss."},{"type":"formula","latex":"P_{\\mathrm{out}}=-130-1.5=-131.5\\text{ dBm}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>out</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>130</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>131.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dBm</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This means that filtering improves frequency selection but may also reduce the power of the useful GNSS signal."}]},{"number":21,"title":"Signal Condition After RF Filtering","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The output is still a continuous analog waveform. Unwanted out-of-band energy has been reduced, but the signal does not automatically become a recognizable GPS waveform."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The spectrum outside the filter's passband is attenuated, while the desired L1 signal spectrum remains within the passed frequency range."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The center frequency is still 1575.42 MHz."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use two frequency-domain plots, before and after filtering, with the same horizontal and vertical scales. Show strong unwanted frequency components being reduced while the wanted L1 band remains."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Underneath, use two corresponding analog time-domain waveforms. Do not display digital samples or a data file at this stage."}]}],"glossary":[["Bandpass Filter","Passes one selected range of frequencies"],["Passband","Frequencies the filter allows through"],["Stopband","Frequencies the filter attenuates"],["Center Frequency","Middle frequency of the passband"],["3-dB Bandwidth","Frequency span defined by the two 3-dB points"],["Insertion Loss","Loss introduced within the wanted passband"],["Selectivity","Ability to distinguish wanted and unwanted frequencies"]]},{"number":5,"title":"Amplification and Noise Figure","slides":[{"number":22,"title":"Why Amplification Is Necessary","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Amplification is the process of increasing the magnitude or power of an electrical signal."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Because the received GNSS signal is extremely weak, amplification is necessary before digitization."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An amplifier increases both the desired signal and the noise already present at its input. Practical amplifiers also add some internal noise."}]},{"number":23,"title":"Understanding Amplifier Gain","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Gain is the ratio of output power to input power."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"When power is expressed in dBm, gain in dB can be added directly:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"G_{\\mathrm{dB}}=P_{\\mathrm{out,dBm}}-P_{\\mathrm{in,dBm}}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mtext>dB</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>out,dBm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>in,dBm</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></math>"},{"type":"heading","text":"Worked example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An amplifier receives \u2212110 dBm and provides 20 dB gain."},{"type":"formula","latex":"P_{\\mathrm{out}}=-110+20=-90\\text{ dBm}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>out</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>110</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>90</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dBm</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The output power is greater than the input power."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"However, amplification does not by itself separate the weak GPS signal from thermal noise."}]},{"number":24,"title":"Understanding Signal-to-Noise Ratio","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) compares the power of a wanted signal with the noise power in the same measurement bandwidth."},{"type":"formula","latex":"SNR=\\frac{P_{\\mathrm{signal}}}{P_{\\mathrm{noise}}}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>signal</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mtext>noise</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A higher SNR means that the signal is stronger relative to the noise."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If both signal and existing noise are amplified by the same amount, their ratio does not improve. If the amplifier adds extra noise, the output SNR becomes worse."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show two time-domain waveforms with different vertical amplitudes but the same relative noise appearance. Show the corresponding frequency spectra with both signal and noise power raised."}]},{"number":25,"title":"Noise Figure","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Noise figure describes how much a receiver component degrades the signal-to-noise ratio."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A lower noise figure indicates less additional degradation."},{"type":"heading","text":"Worked example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A component receives a signal with an input SNR of 10 dB and produces an output SNR of 7 dB."},{"type":"formula","latex":"NF=10-7=3\\text{ dB}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>dB</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Therefore, the component has introduced a 3 dB deterioration in SNR."}]},{"number":26,"title":"Why the First Amplifier Is Important","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The total receiver noise figure depends strongly on the first stages in the front end."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For cascaded components, the noise factor is expressed by the Friis equation:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"F_{\\mathrm{system}}\n=\nF_1+\\frac{F_2-1}{G_1}\n+\\frac{F_3-1}{G_1G_2}+\\cdots","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>system</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mo>\u22ef</mo></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, \\(F\\) represents noise factor and \\(G\\) represents linear power gain."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The equation shows that the noise contributed by later stages is reduced in relative importance when the first amplifier provides sufficiently high gain."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Compare an 8 dB cable loss placed before an LNA with the same cable placed after a 30 dB low-noise amplifier."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The second arrangement provides better protection of receiver sensitivity."}]},{"number":27,"title":"Signal Condition After Amplification","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The analog voltage becomes larger, but its noise-like character remains. It is still not a decoded GNSS signal."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The desired GPS signal and the existing input noise both increase in power. The frequency center remains at 1575.42 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Practical amplifier noise adds to the spectrum, and no frequency translation has yet occurred."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show an analog waveform before and after amplification, using equal scales. Show that the amplitude increases without changing the frequency of the carrier."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"On the frequency plots, show that the spectrum is raised in power without shifting along the frequency axis."}]}],"glossary":[["Amplification","Increase in signal magnitude or power"],["Gain","Ratio of output power to input power"],["SNR","Signal power relative to noise power"],["Noise Figure","SNR degradation introduced by a component"],["Noise Factor","Linear representation of noise figure"],["Friis Equation","Equation describing cascaded receiver noise"],["Cable Loss","Reduction in signal power through a cable"]]},{"number":6,"title":"Mixer and Local Oscillator","slides":[{"number":28,"title":"Why Frequency Translation Is Necessary","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The received GPS L1 signal is centered at 1575.42 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For the reference receiver, processing this signal is made more practical by translating it to a lower frequency before the ADC."},{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Frequency translation is the process of shifting the frequency of a signal while preserving its useful modulation information."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The lower frequency produced by this operation is called the intermediate frequency (IF)."}]},{"number":29,"title":"Function of the Mixer","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A mixer combines the incoming RF signal with a signal generated by the local oscillator (LO)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The mixing operation produces sum-frequency and difference-frequency components."},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{difference}}=|f_{\\mathrm{RF}}-f_{\\mathrm{LO}}|","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>difference</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>RF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>LO</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{sum}}=f_{\\mathrm{RF}}+f_{\\mathrm{LO}}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>sum</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>RF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>LO</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The desired difference-frequency component can then be selected by an IF filter."}]},{"number":30,"title":"Worked Example: GPS L1 Frequency Conversion","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Given"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{RF}}=1575.42\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>RF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1575.42</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{LO}}=1527.68\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>LO</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1527.68</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"heading","text":"Difference frequency"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{IF}}=1575.42-1527.68","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>IF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1575.42</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1527.68</mn></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"\\boxed{f_{\\mathrm{IF}}=47.74\\text{ MHz}}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>IF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>47.74</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>"},{"type":"heading","text":"Sum frequency"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{sum}}=1575.42+1527.68","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>sum</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1575.42</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1527.68</mn></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"\\boxed{f_{\\mathrm{sum}}=3103.10\\text{ MHz}}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>sum</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3103.10</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The next filter retains the desired 47.74 MHz component while attenuating the unwanted high-frequency mixing product."}]},{"number":31,"title":"Role of the Local Oscillator","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A local oscillator generates the stable periodic signal used by the mixer to perform frequency translation."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In the reference front end, its frequency is 1527.68 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A stable receiver clock and frequency synthesizer are used to generate the required frequencies for the local oscillator and sampling operations."},{"type":"heading","text":"Reference values"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Receiver clock: 10 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Synthesized local oscillator: 1527.68 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"ADC sampling frequency: 38.192 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The sampling frequency is obtained in the reference design using a divider:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_s=\\frac{1527.68}{40}=38.192\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1527.68</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>38.192</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"}]},{"number":32,"title":"Understanding Mixer Image Frequency","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An image frequency is an unwanted RF frequency that produces the same difference-frequency magnitude as the desired RF signal when mixed with the local oscillator."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For the reference design:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{image}}=1527.68-47.74","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>image</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1527.68</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>47.74</mn></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{image}}=1479.94\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>image</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1479.94</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Both 1479.94 MHz and 1575.42 MHz can produce a 47.74 MHz difference relative to the selected local oscillator."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is one reason why RF filtering is important before mixing."}]},{"number":33,"title":"Signal Condition After Mixing","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The signal remains analog. Its carrier frequency has been shifted from 1575.42 MHz to 47.74 MHz, while its ranging-code and navigation-data modulation is preserved."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In a real noise-dominated recording, the waveform continues to appear irregular."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Immediately after mixing, both the sum and difference components may be present."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The desired component is centered at 47.74 MHz. The unwanted sum component is centered at 3103.10 MHz."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show a frequency-axis diagram with an input band centered at 1575.42 MHz and the two resulting mixer components. Emphasize the desired 47.74 MHz output."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use a separate idealized waveform to illustrate the reduced carrier frequency, with a caption stating that actual received GNSS signals remain noise-like."}]}],"glossary":[["Mixer","Component that combines RF and LO signals"],["Local Oscillator","Stable signal generator used for mixing"],["Frequency Translation","Shifting a signal to another frequency"],["Intermediate Frequency","Frequency obtained after conversion"],["Difference Frequency","Magnitude of RF frequency minus LO frequency"],["Sum Frequency","RF frequency plus LO frequency"],["Image Frequency","Unwanted RF input that maps to the same IF"],["Frequency Synthesizer","Circuit generating a required frequency from a reference"]]},{"number":7,"title":"Intermediate-Frequency Filtering and Amplification","slides":[{"number":34,"title":"Why IF Filtering Is Required","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The mixer output contains the desired intermediate-frequency signal and unwanted mixing components."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An IF bandpass filter selects the desired frequency region."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Filtering at 47.74 MHz also permits practical control of the signal bandwidth before digitization."}]},{"number":35,"title":"First IF Bandpass Filter","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference first IF filter has:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_c=47.74\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>47.74</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"BW\\approx18\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>18</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Its primary role is to retain the desired IF band while attenuating unwanted mixing products and out-of-band energy."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show the mixer spectrum before filtering and the IF spectrum after filtering."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The 47.74 MHz band remains; the high-frequency sum product is removed."}]},{"number":36,"title":"IF Amplifier","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The IF amplifier increases the magnitude of the intermediate-frequency signal before the ADC."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference lecture describes an IF amplifier providing approximately 50 dB gain."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Like the RF amplifier, it increases the desired signal and existing noise, while adding some internal noise."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An analog IF signal enters the amplifier at a low amplitude. The amplifier increases its magnitude so that the later ADC can use its available input voltage range effectively."}]},{"number":37,"title":"Final IF Bandpass Filter","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference final IF filter has:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_c=47.74\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>47.74</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"BW\\approx6\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>6</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Its role is to limit the analog bandwidth before sampling, reducing unwanted frequency components and integrated noise."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS L1 C/A main lobe occupies approximately 2.046 MHz, so this 6 MHz filter passes the main lobe while also retaining part of the surrounding side-lobe spectrum."}]},{"number":38,"title":"Why Filters Are Used at Different Frequencies","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The same narrow absolute bandwidth is generally easier to implement at a lower center frequency."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The quality factor is:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"Q=\\frac{f_{\\mathrm{center}}}{BW}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>center</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>W</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For a bandwidth of 2.046 MHz at the GPS L1 RF frequency:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"Q_{\\mathrm{RF}}=\\frac{1575.42}{2.046}\\approx770","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>RF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1575.42</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2.046</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>770</mn></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"At the 47.74 MHz IF:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"Q_{\\mathrm{IF}}=\\frac{47.74}{2.046}\\approx23.33","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>IF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>47.74</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2.046</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>23.33</mn></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This comparison illustrates why narrow filtering can be more practical after frequency translation."}]},{"number":39,"title":"Signal Condition After IF Conditioning","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The signal remains a continuous analog waveform. The signal amplitude has been adjusted, and unwanted components have been attenuated."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The original PRN modulation and navigation data are still present."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The desired signal is centered at 47.74 MHz, within the bandwidth selected by the final IF filter."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The frequency center does not change during IF filtering or amplification."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show three frequency plots using the same scales:"},{"type":"bullet","text":"After mixer: desired IF plus unwanted products."},{"type":"bullet","text":"After first IF filter: desired IF with reduced unwanted products."},{"type":"bullet","text":"After final IF filter: narrower passed band around 47.74 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show a noise-like analog waveform underneath each plot, emphasizing that the signal has not yet been digitized."}]}],"glossary":[["IF","Intermediate Frequency"],["IF Filter","Frequency-selective component operating at IF"],["IF Amplifier","Amplifier operating at intermediate frequency"],["Quality Factor","Ratio of filter center frequency to bandwidth"],["Analog Bandwidth","Frequency range handled by the analog circuit"],["Filtering","Reduction of unwanted frequency components"]]},{"number":8,"title":"Analog-to-Digital Converter","slides":[{"number":40,"title":"What Is an Analog-to-Digital Converter?","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) is a circuit that converts a continuous analog electrical signal into a sequence of digital numbers."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The ADC performs two fundamental operations:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sampling: Measuring the signal amplitude at selected instants of time."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Quantization: Assigning each measured amplitude to one of a finite number of digital levels."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The ADC therefore changes the representation of the signal, not its underlying satellite information."}]},{"number":41,"title":"Understanding Sampling","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sampling is the process of measuring the amplitude of an analog signal at regular time intervals."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The number of samples taken each second is called the sampling frequency."},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_s=\\frac{1}{T_s}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, \\(T_s\\) is the time interval between successive samples."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show a continuous analog sinusoidal curve with small dots placed at equal time intervals. Each dot represents a measured amplitude."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Explain that the dots are measurements of the original waveform, not a separate signal transmitted by the satellite."}]},{"number":42,"title":"Sampling Frequency Example","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference GPS front end uses:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_s=38.192\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>38.192</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This means:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"38.192\\times10^6","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>38.192</mn><mo>\u00d7</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"samples are taken every second."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The time interval between samples is:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"T_s=\\frac{1}{38.192\\times10^6}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>38.192</mn><mo>\u00d7</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"T_s\\approx26.18\\text{ ns}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>26.18</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>ns</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Therefore, the ADC measures the incoming analog signal approximately once every 26.18 nanoseconds."}]},{"number":43,"title":"Nyquist Frequency","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Nyquist frequency is half of the sampling frequency."},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_N=\\frac{f_s}{2}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For the reference design:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_N=\\frac{38.192}{2}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>38.192</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"\\boxed{f_N=19.096\\text{ MHz}}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>19.096</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In ordinary sampling without intended aliasing, frequencies above the Nyquist limit can become indistinguishable from lower frequencies in the sampled representation."}]},{"number":44,"title":"Understanding Aliasing","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Aliasing occurs when different analog frequencies produce indistinguishable sampled-frequency representations."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This can happen when the sampling rate is insufficient to uniquely represent the analog frequencies involved."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In the reference GPS front end, aliasing is used intentionally to represent the 47.74 MHz analog IF at a lower digital frequency."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show two simple sinusoidal waveforms sampled at the same instants, producing the same pattern of sample points despite their different analog frequencies."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is an introductory demonstration; the actual GPS signal is spread-spectrum and noise-dominated."}]},{"number":45,"title":"Worked Example: Intentional Aliasing","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Given"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Analog IF:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{IF}}=47.74\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>IF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>47.74</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sampling frequency:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_s=38.192\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>38.192</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference design produces:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{digital}}=|f_{\\mathrm{IF}}-f_s|","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>digital</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>IF</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{digital}}=|47.74-38.192|","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>digital</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>47.74</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>38.192</mn><mo>|</mo></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"\\boxed{f_{\\mathrm{digital}}=9.548\\text{ MHz}}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>digital</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>9.548</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The signal's center frequency appears at 9.548 MHz in the sampled spectrum."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The desired main-lobe bandwidth remains approximately 2.046 MHz, provided that the analog filtering and sampling conditions preserve it."}]},{"number":46,"title":"Signal in the Time and Frequency Domains After Sampling","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The signal is no longer represented by a continuously available voltage measurement. It is represented at discrete instants."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The recorded GPS samples still appear irregular because the input is noise dominated."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The 47.74 MHz analog IF band appears at a digital frequency of 9.548 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS C/A main lobe appears approximately from 8.525 MHz to 10.571 MHz."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Display a before-and-after comparison with the analog IF spectrum centered at 47.74 MHz and the sampled spectrum centered at 9.548 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use the same approximate 2.046 MHz main-lobe width in both plots."}]},{"number":47,"title":"Understanding Quantization","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Quantization is the process of representing each sampled analog amplitude using one of a fixed number of digital levels."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Analog voltage can vary continuously within the input range. Digital representation uses only a limited set of values."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The number of quantization levels is determined by the number of bits:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"L=2^N","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"where \\(N\\) is the number of bits and \\(L\\) is the number of available levels."}]},{"number":48,"title":"Quantization Bits and Levels","blocks":[{"type":"table","rows":[["Bit Depth","Number of Levels"],["1 bit","2"],["2 bits","4"],["3 bits","8"],["4 bits","16"],["8 bits","256"]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference ADC has 4-bit resolution and therefore provides:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"2^4=16","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"possible digital amplitude levels."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A larger number of bits allows amplitudes to be represented in smaller steps for a fixed analog input range."}]},{"number":49,"title":"Visual Example: Four-Bit Quantization Bins","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A quantization bin is one of the intervals into which the ADC's analog input range is divided. Each bin is associated with one digital output level."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"4-Bit ADC \u2014 Sixteen Quantization Bins"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The 16 bins shown are equally spaced for a uniform 4-bit ADC. Their corresponding analog voltage boundaries depend on the ADC input range."},{"type":"heading","text":"Explanation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"When an analog sample falls within a particular bin, the ADC assigns its corresponding digital level."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, the ADC may assign an analog measurement falling in bin 8 to output level 8."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Each sample is therefore represented by one of the 16 available amplitude levels."}]},{"number":50,"title":"Sampling Versus Quantization","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"These operations must be clearly distinguished."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sampling answers: At what times do we measure the waveform?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Quantization answers: What digital amplitude value do we assign to each measurement?"},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show the same signal in three successive stages:"},{"type":"numbered","text":"Continuous analog waveform."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Analog measurements marked at equal time intervals."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Measured points assigned to available digital amplitude levels."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The third display should resemble a stepped collection of digital amplitude values rather than a continuous analog curve."}]},{"number":51,"title":"Automatic Gain Control","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Automatic Gain Control (AGC) adjusts the amplification applied to the signal so that the ADC's input voltage range is used appropriately."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If the signal entering the ADC is too small, only a few central quantization levels may be used."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If the signal is too large, the ADC cannot represent amplitudes beyond its range. This is known as clipping or saturation."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show three cases:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Input Condition\tQuantization Result"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Gain too low\tMost samples occupy a few middle levels"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Appropriate gain\tSamples occupy a useful range of levels"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Gain too high\tMany samples accumulate at the highest and lowest levels"}]},{"number":52,"title":"Signal Condition After Digitization","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Time-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The waveform is represented by discrete numerical amplitude values rather than a continuously varying voltage."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The sample values can only occupy the finite levels supported by the ADC."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency-domain condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The sampled signal is represented around a digital IF of 9.548 MHz for the reference design."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The wanted GNSS modulation is retained, while the sample representation also contains noise and any residual interference."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The ADC does not identify satellites or remove the thermal noise."}]}],"glossary":[["ADC","Analog-to-Digital Converter"],["Sampling","Measuring amplitude at selected times"],["Sampling Frequency","Number of samples per second"],["Sampling Interval","Time between samples"],["Nyquist Frequency","Half the sampling frequency"],["Aliasing","Ambiguity between analog and sampled frequencies"],["Quantization","Assigning amplitude to digital levels"],["Bit Depth","Number of bits used for each sample"],["Quantization Bin","Interval associated with one digital output level"],["Clipping","Limiting amplitudes beyond the ADC range"],["AGC","Automatic Gain Control"]]},{"number":9,"title":"Resulting Digital GNSS Signal","slides":[{"number":53,"title":"The Output of the GPS Front End","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"At the output of the ADC, the reference GPS front end produces digital IF samples described by three essential parameters:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_s=38.192\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>38.192</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"f_{\\mathrm{digital}}=9.548\\text{ MHz}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>digital</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>9.548</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>MHz</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"N=4\\text{ bits per sample}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mtext>bits per sample</mtext></mrow></math>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These samples contain the received composite GNSS signal together with noise."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The receiver can now analyze them using digital signal-processing methods."}]},{"number":54,"title":"Time-Domain Representation After Digitization","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A digital time-domain plot shows the recorded sample amplitude against time or sample number."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The time-domain recording of a real GNSS signal is normally noise-like. Even though the digital IF is 9.548 MHz, the waveform does not necessarily show a clearly distinguishable sinusoid."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Suppose 1,000 samples are displayed."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"At 38.192 million samples per second, the displayed time span is approximately:"},{"type":"formula","latex":"T=\\frac{1000}{38.192\\times10^6}","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>38.192</mn><mo>\u00d7</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>"},{"type":"formula","latex":"T\\approx26.2\\ \\mu s","html":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>\u2248</mo><mn>26.2</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"/><mo>\u03bc</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>"},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Plot 1,000 discrete amplitude values with sample number on the horizontal axis and quantized amplitude on the vertical axis. Show the rapid irregular variation expected in a noise-dominated capture."}]},{"number":55,"title":"Histogram of Digitized Samples","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A histogram shows how many digital samples occupy each available amplitude level."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For a 4-bit ADC, there are 16 possible levels."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For a noise-dominated GNSS input with appropriate gain, the histogram will often resemble a bell-shaped distribution."},{"type":"heading","text":"Example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Suppose 1,048,576 samples are recorded."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The histogram counts how many of these samples were assigned to each of the 16 amplitude levels."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The total count across all bins must equal the number of recorded samples."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Illustrative Histogram \u2014 4-Bit Quantized Samples"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Conceptual, not measured data. Middle levels contain more samples than extreme levels, illustrating a typical noise-dominated amplitude distribution."},{"type":"heading","text":"Interpretation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A balanced, centrally concentrated histogram indicates that the ADC levels are being used without excessive clipping."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A large accumulation at the extreme bins can indicate excessive gain."}]},{"number":56,"title":"Frequency-Domain Representation After Digitization","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Definition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The frequency-domain representation shows how power is distributed over the frequencies present in the digital sample sequence."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The reference output has a digital IF centered at approximately 9.548 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The GPS L1 C/A main-lobe bandwidth is approximately 2.046 MHz."},{"type":"heading","text":"Visual example"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Show the sampled spectrum with a frequency axis, a noise floor, and a central signal band near 9.548 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The desired central main lobe may appear as a broad rise in the spectrum when the combined received GNSS power and observation conditions make it visible."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The important point is that the frequency-domain representation can help identify the signal band, unwanted interference, and the effect of receiver filtering."}]},{"number":57,"title":"Why the Received Samples Still Look Like Noise","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The received GPS signal remains weak relative to thermal noise."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The antenna, filters, amplifiers, mixer, and ADC do not directly extract the PRN code from that noise."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The visible time-domain waveform can therefore appear random even when valid GNSS information is present."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The next receiver stages use the known satellite ranging codes to identify and process the received signals."},{"type":"heading","text":"Interpretation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An irregular sampled waveform does not, by itself, mean that GNSS reception has failed."}]},{"number":58,"title":"Three Representations of the Same Digitized Signal","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is an important final visualization. Display all three representations simultaneously."},{"type":"heading","text":"Time Domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Shows amplitude variation and clipping."},{"type":"heading","text":"Histogram"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Shows how the quantization levels are occupied."},{"type":"heading","text":"Frequency Domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Shows spectral content, including a possible main-lobe rise near the digital IF."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Each graph answers a different question."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Time domain: How do sample amplitudes change with time?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Histogram: How frequently is each digital amplitude level used?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Frequency domain: How is signal and noise power distributed over frequency?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These are three representations of the same digitized signal, not three different output signals."}]}],"glossary":[["Digital IF","Digitized representation of the intermediate-frequency signal"],["Digital Sample","Numerical representation of a measured signal amplitude"],["Time-Domain Plot","Sample amplitude against time or sample number"],["Histogram","Number of samples in each amplitude level"],["Frequency Spectrum","Signal power distribution with frequency"],["PSD","Power Spectral Density"],["Sample Count","Number of recorded digital measurements"]]},{"number":10,"title":"Complete Front-End Signal Transformation","slides":[{"number":59,"title":"What Happens to the Signal at Every Stage?","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"This slide should summarize the entire lecture through one continuous comparison."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Front-End Stage\tSignal in Time Domain\tSignal in Frequency Domain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"GNSS Antenna\tExtremely weak, analog, noise-like voltage\tReceived GPS L1 spectrum around 1575.42 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"RF Filter\tAnalog signal with reduced unwanted energy\tOut-of-band components attenuated"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"RF Amplifier\tIncreased analog amplitude, still noise-like\tSignal and existing noise power increased; center unchanged"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Mixer\tAnalog frequency-converted signal\tDesired 47.74 MHz IF plus unwanted mixer products"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"First IF Filter\tAnalog signal with unwanted mixing components reduced\tIF band retained around 47.74 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"IF Amplifier\tIncreased IF signal amplitude\tIF signal and noise power increased"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Final IF Filter\tBand-limited analog IF waveform\tApproximately 6 MHz passband centered at 47.74 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"ADC Sampling\tDiscrete measurements in time\tDesired band aliases to 9.548 MHz"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"ADC Quantization\tFinite amplitude levels\tDigitized IF spectrum with quantization effects"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Digital Output\tNoise-like sampled waveform with 16 possible levels\tSampled GNSS spectrum centered near 9.548 MHz"}]},{"number":60,"title":"Final Worked Example: Follow One GPS L1 Signal","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"A GPS satellite transmits a code-modulated signal centered at 1575.42 MHz."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The antenna receives the weak signal and converts it into an electrical voltage. The front end then performs the following operations."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Reception: The antenna receives the weak GPS L1 signal, which is below the thermal noise floor in the reference example."},{"type":"numbered","text":"RF Filtering: The filter selects the wanted L1 band and attenuates unwanted out-of-band energy."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Amplification: The RF amplifier increases the weak signal magnitude while adding as little extra noise as practical."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Frequency Translation: The mixer combines the 1575.42 MHz RF signal with the 1527.68 MHz local oscillator, producing a desired 47.74 MHz IF."},{"type":"numbered","text":"IF Conditioning: Filtering and amplification prepare the analog signal for the ADC."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Sampling: The ADC measures the signal at 38.192 million samples per second. The desired IF appears at 9.548 MHz in the sampled spectrum."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Quantization: Each measured amplitude is represented using one of 16 possible levels in the 4-bit ADC."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Digital Output: The sampled signal can be represented by a time-domain waveform, amplitude histogram, and frequency-domain spectrum."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The final output is a digital representation of the combined received GNSS signals, suitable for subsequent acquisition and tracking."}]},{"number":61,"title":"Final Conceptual Review","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Students should be able to answer the following questions after completing the lecture."},{"type":"numbered","text":"Why is the GPS L1 signal difficult to identify in the received time-domain waveform?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"What is the difference between the 1575.42 MHz carrier frequency and the approximately 2.046 MHz main-lobe bandwidth?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"Why does a GNSS receiver require both RF filtering and amplification?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"Why is low-noise amplification important before large cable losses?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"What does a mixer do, and why is a local oscillator required?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"How is the 47.74 MHz analog IF obtained?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"Why are additional IF filters required?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"What is the difference between sampling and quantization?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"How does the 47.74 MHz IF appear at 9.548 MHz after sampling?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"Why does a 4-bit ADC have 16 quantization levels?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"How does the histogram reveal insufficient gain or clipping?"},{"type":"numbered","text":"Why can a correctly recorded GNSS signal still look like random noise?"}]}],"glossary":[]}],"source_sha256":"ec2ec85608d4ffb1ccbaa97c947ebc05e13635d447a64ba236fddab7eda9e207"};
